      "id": 1,
      "text": "Sharon Hill became an independent borough in 1890 after breaking away from Darby.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://findingaids.library.upenn.edu/records/SMREP_SHHS.02",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The arrival of the Philadelphia, Wilmington, and Baltimore Railroad in 1872 turned Sharon Hill from undeveloped land into a growing residential and commercial hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://findingaids.library.upenn.edu/records/SMREP_SHHS.02",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Sharon Hill Historical Society’s 1879‑2013 collection includes photographs, maps, and oral histories that chronicle the borough’s evolution.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://findingaids.library.upenn.edu/records/SMREP_SHHS.02",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The borough’s name honors an estate called “Sharon” that once spanned nearly 75 acres of the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://sharonhillboro.com/history/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Academy Park High School now stands where John Jackson’s early 1800s Sharon Female Seminary once taught girls.",
